// SPDX-License-Identifier: UNLICENSED
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;
import {Test} from "forge-std/Test.sol";
import {C} from "../src/C_under_test.sol";

contract InvMutTest is Test {
    C c;
    function setUp() public { c = new C(); }
    function testRegression_property() public {
        // Set MinSum to 1 wei so that Collect can succeed
        c.SetMinSum(1);
        // Set log file address (any address works for this test)
        c.SetLogFile(address(0x123));
        // Initialize the contract so that SetLogFile can no longer be called
        c.Initialized();
        // Deploy 1 ether to user address
        address user = address(0x456);
        vm.deal(user, 1 ether);
        vm.prank(user);
        c.Put{value: 1 ether}(0);
        // Warp past unlockTime
        vm.warp(block.timestamp + 1);
        // Now try to call Collect; in reference, Collect will revert because Log is not set (due to Initialized preventing SetLogFile), while in mutant SetLogFile succeeded so Collect works.
        // Actually, the property says P.SetLogFile() != M.SetLogFile() after Collect. We check that in reference SetLogFile returns address(0) (since SetLogFile was called before Initialized but Log is set correctly? Wait, need to re-evaluate the difference.
        // The mutation changes SetLogFile to always succeed even after Initialized. So in P, after Initialized, SetLogFile reverts; in M, it succeeds.
        // But the problem statement: after Collect completes, SetLogFile differs. That means we need to call SetLogFile after Initialized, which will revert in P but succeed in M. Then Collect will see different Log.
        // Reset scenario: 
        // 1) SetMinSum(1)
        // 2) SetLogFile(addr) - this works in both
        // 3) Initialized()
        // 4) Now SetLogFile again to a different address. In P this reverts, in M it succeeds, changing Log.
        // But SetLogFile is not part of the difference described; the difference is in the Collect path. The simplest witness: after Initialized, call SetLogFile to a new address; in P it reverts so Log stays old, in M it succeeds so Log changes. Then call Collect; Collect uses Log.AddMessage. If Log is different, the behavior in AddMessage differs? Actually no, AddMessage just logs. The property says after Collect, P.SetLogFile() != M.SetLogFile(). That means the state variable SetLogFile (the getter c.SetLogFile()) differs.
        // The getter c.SetLogFile() returns the current value of the public state variable SetLogFile (address). In reference, after Initialized, SetLogFile cannot be changed, so it remains the old address. In mutant, it can be changed, so it becomes the new address. So we just need to call SetLogFile after Initialized and check that in reference it reverts (so value unchanged) and in mutant it changes.
        // But the requirement says "driven through the public/external entry Collect(uint256)". So the divergence must be triggered by calling Collect. That means we need a scenario where Collect behaves differently due to the Log address difference, and after Collect, we observe that c.SetLogFile() differs.
        // Actually, the problem statement: "after a call to Collect(...) completes, the value of state variable SetLogFile differs: P.SetLogFile() != M.SetLogFile()."
        // That means Collect must itself change SetLogFile? No, SetLogFile is only set by SetLogFile function. Unless Collect somehow calls SetLogFile? It doesn't. So the only way for SetLogFile to differ after Collect is if SetLogFile was called before Collect and the mutation caused it to succeed/fail differently. So the call sequence must include SetLogFile before Collect, and the mutation makes that SetLogFile call succeed in M but revert in P.
        // Therefore: 
        // - deploy C
        // - SetMinSum(1)
        // - Initialized()
        // - Then call SetLogFile with a new address; in P it reverts (initialized is true), in M it succeeds.
        // - Then fund user, Put, warp, Collect (Collect will use Log which differs).
